{"tool_name":"deconstructsigs","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"deconstructsigs","home":"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/deconstructSigs/index.html","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Takes sample information in the form of the fraction of mutations in each of 96 trinucleotide contexts and identifies the weighted combination of published signatures that, when summed, most closely reconstructs the mutational profile.","addition_date":"2018-08-12T09:57:15Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:28:16.769479Z","tool_type":["Library"],"version":["1.8.0"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:deconstructsigs","credit":[{"email":"rachel.rosenthal.14@ucl.ac.uk","name":"Rachel Rosenthal","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/deconstructSigs/deconstructSigs.pdf"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Pathway or network prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3439"}]}],"name":"deconstructSigs","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s13059-016-0893-4","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: Analysis of somatic mutations provides insight into the mutational processes that have shaped the cancer genome, but such analysis currently requires large cohorts. We develop deconstructSigs, which allows the identification of mutational signatures within a single tumor sample. Results: Application of deconstructSigs identifies samples with DNA repair deficiencies and reveals distinct and dynamic mutational processes molding the cancer genome in esophageal adenocarcinoma compared to squamous cell carcinomas. Conclusions: deconstructSigs confers the ability to define mutational processes driven by environmental exposures, DNA repair abnormalities, and mutagenic processes in individual tumors with implications for precision cancer medicine.","authors":